1. Why Anomaly Detection? (Reactive vs. Proactive)
If you only build a system that handles user queries, you have built a Chatbot. It is reactive. The system breaks, the user notices, the user asks the bot, and the bot fixes it.

Anomaly Detection makes the system Proactive.

The Role: The Isolation Forest (Anomaly Detection) acts as the system s "heart rate monitor." It constantly watches the server logs in the background.

The Magic: If the CPU spikes to 99% at 2:00 AM, the user is asleep. The Anomaly Detection model notices the spike and automatically triggers the AI Agent. The Agent reads the logs, finds the infinite loop in the code, and restarts the server—all before the user wakes up.

Interview Answer: "Without anomaly detection, the AI is blind until a human talks to it. With it, I built a truly Autonomous Self-Healing System."

2. Why Do We Need Docker? (Safety & Sandboxing)
You are giving an AI the ability to execute terminal commands (the "Hands"). LLMs hallucinate.

If the AI decides the best way to fix a disk space error is to run rm -rf / (delete everything) directly on your laptop, it will destroy your operating system.

The Role: Docker acts as a Sandbox.

The Magic: Every time the AI wants to run a command (like pip install or running a python script to test a fix), your backend spins up a temporary, isolated Docker container. The AI runs its commands inside that container. If the AI breaks it, who cares? You just delete the container, and your actual computer remains 100% safe.

Interview Answer: "I used Docker SDK to sandbox the AI's execution environment. Giving an LLM direct root access to a host machine is a massive security vulnerability. Docker ensures the AI can test its code fixes in a zero-risk, ephemeral environment."